      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-32-2020-october/q5",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:80mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-32-2020-october/q05-figure.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2: a ball projected from O at the top of a vertical cliff with speed U m/s at 45 degrees above the horizontal; O is 25 m above N on horizontal ground, and the ball lands at A where NA = 100 m.\"></figure><p>A small ball is projected with speed <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"U\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\"></span> from a point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> at the top of a vertical cliff.</p><p>The point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"25\\,\\mathrm m\"></span> vertically above the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"N\"></span> which is on horizontal ground.</p><p>The ball is projected at an angle of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"45^\\circ\"></span> above the horizontal.</p><p>The ball hits the ground at a point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"AN=100\\,\\mathrm m\"></span>, as shown in Figure 2.</p><p>The motion of the ball is modelled as that of a particle moving freely under gravity.</p><p>Using this initial model,</p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"U=28\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></li><li>find the greatest height of the ball above the horizontal ground <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"NA\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li></ol><p>In a refinement to the model of the motion of the ball from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span>, the effect of air resistance is included.</p><p>This refined model is used to find a new value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"U\"></span>.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"3\"><li>How would this new value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"U\"></span> compare with 28, the value given in part (a)? <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li><li>State one further refinement to the model that would make the model more realistic. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li></ol>",
